{"_comment": "Non-resident graduate degrees and nonimmigrant visa issuances on one time axis, divided into named windows. PHASE BOUNDARIES ARE A JUDGMENT, not a result: they are set from turning points in the series and from known external events, and are listed with the reason for each so a reader can move them. Growth inside each window is computed. THE LAG IS TESTED ON DIFFERENCED SERIES -- year-over-year changes, which removes the shared trend that would make any two rising series correlate -- with 2020-21 excluded as an exogenous consular shock. It peaks at three years and sits near zero at lag zero, which is the shape a mechanical lag produces and not the shape a common trend produces. IT IS STILL A CORRELATION on 16 observations and is consistent with the mechanics of a two-year degree rather than proof of them. DEGREES AND ISSUANCES ARE DIFFERENT UNITS: an issuance is a document and not a person, a degree is a conferral and not a person, and neither is a headcount of anyone.", "sources": {"degrees": "NCES IPEDS Completions, eight core-STEM CIP families", "visas": "US Department of State, nonimmigrant visa detail tables"}, "shock_years_excluded_from_trends": [2020, 2021], "phases": [{"start": 1998, "end": 2003, "name": "Post-9/11 contraction", "why": "Student visa issuance falls after the 2001 attacks and the consular tightening that followed.", "series": {"masters_nonresident_degrees": {"first_year": 1998, "last_year": 2003, "first": 18678, "last": 28222, "change_pct": 51.1, "peak_year": 2003, "peak": 28222, "peak_change_pct": 51.1, "annualised_pct": 8.6}, "masters_nonresident_share": {"first_year": 1998, "last_year": 2003, "first": 26.2, "last": 32.1, "change_pct": 22.5, "peak_year": 2003, "peak": 32.1, "peak_change_pct": 22.5, "annualised_pct": 4.1}, "doctorate_nonresident_degrees": {"first_year": 1998, "last_year": 2003, "first": 6315, "last": 7088, "change_pct": 12.2, "peak_year": 2003, "peak": 7088, "peak_change_pct": 12.2, "annualised_pct": 2.3}, "f1_i